=== REPORT ===
Built `qwen_task.py` (358 lines) — long-term statistics of tasks delegated to the local Qwen, in the style of the reference `qwen_savings.py` (module docstring with purpose/formulas/usage, small functions, stdlib only, plotly imported lazily in `write_html`).

Implemented exactly the specified API:
- Call-time paths via env: `stats_dir()`, `calls_file()`, `tasks_file()`, `current_file()`, `html_file()`, `transcripts_dir()`.
- `newest_transcript()` (newest `root/*/*.jsonl` by mtime), `claude_usage()` (assistant lines in [start, end] UTC, one count per `message.id`, invalid lines skipped, `cache_creation` detail split 5m/1h with fallback to 1h, `turns/tokens/usd` from PRICES), `current_task()`, `start()`, `end()` (record with the exact 17 keys in order, note truncated to 200, single JSON line appended, one-line `[qwen task]` report with cumulative totals), `read_jsonl()`, `summary()`, `write_html()`, `main()` CLI (start/end/status/report).
- HTML: 5-row subplot grid (cumulative tasks & Qwen calls over time; per-task Qwen minutes; per-task Claude tokens stacked input/output/cache-read/cache-write in a separate row — the installed plotly version lacks `add_yaxis`, so the allowed "separate row" option was used; cumulative $ saving/Claude cost/net; tasks per outcome) + a `go.Table` of all tasks in a second figure, both rendered into one self-contained page (`include_plotlyjs="cdn"`, French titles, `plotly_white`). Works with zero tasks and zero calls.

Verification:
- `python -m pytest -q tests` → 3 passed (all acceptance tests).
- CLI smoke test via subprocess with redirected env dirs (start/status/end with a logged call/report/status) → all commands succeeded, HTML generated and contains the task title and plotly.
- Scratch file deleted; tests left unmodified.

Not done / limitations: file is 358 lines, slightly above the ~300 soft target (kept small functions for readability); the HTML combines two figures (main grid + table) into one page because `Figure.append`/table-in-subplots is not available in this plotly version.

